WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.760
CI/CD compromise can turn the software delivery pipeline into an attack path.

00:00:04.980 --> 00:00:10.340
Source control permissions, build runners, deployment tokens, artifact repositories,

00:00:10.560 --> 00:00:12.640
and release workflows need protection.

00:00:12.860 --> 00:00:17.500
Unsigned code and unverified artifacts make it difficult to prove that deployed software

00:00:17.720 --> 00:00:19.960
is authentic and unchanged.

00:00:20.180 --> 00:00:24.180
Integrity validation should cover the path from source to build to release.

00:00:24.400 --> 00:00:29.520
Insecure deserialization occurs when applications trust serialized input to reconstruct

00:00:29.740 --> 00:00:31.580
objects or behavior.

00:00:31.800 --> 00:00:36.440
Attackers may manipulate serialized data to alter state, bypass controls,

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:38.740
or trigger unsafe server behavior.

00:00:38.960 --> 00:00:43.520
Build pipeline attacks are especially important for SaaS providers because a compromised

00:00:43.740 --> 00:00:46.140
pipeline can affect many customers at once.

00:00:46.360 --> 00:00:51.240
Change management, approvals, and monitoring help keep delivery trustworthy.

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:55.220
Use code signing and artifact integrity validation.

00:00:55.440 --> 00:00:59.680
Secure CI/CD with least privilege, approvals, and monitored changes.

00:00:59.900 --> 00:01:03.660
Restrict and review changes to production software and data flows.

00:01:03.880 --> 00:01:09.560
Selected SOC 2 mappings for this topic include CC7.3 - Integrity monitoring;

00:01:09.780 --> 00:01:12.980
CC8.1 - Change management controls.
